+# Contract: Session Hook End Flow (script ↔ host ↔ Runtime)
+
+**Feature**: [../spec.md](../spec.md) | **Surface**: the command-hook interface of
+`session_hook.py` — what the host invokes, what the script sends to the Runtime, and what it may
+report. The hook is a client of the Runtime's public HTTP contract; no Runtime surface changes in
+this feature.
+
+## Invocation (host → hook)
+
+```text
+python <plugin>/hooks/session_hook.py end --host <claude|codex>
+stdin:  {"hook_event_name": "SessionEnd", "session_id": "<id>", ...}   (≤ 64 KiB)
+exit:   0 for every observed-or-unobserved outcome of `end`
+stdout: empty on `end` (success and unobserved alike)
+stderr: "graphhelm-hook: end unobserved (<ErrorType>)" when the operation is unobserved
+```
+
+Unbound sessions (no `GRAPHHELM_EXECUTION_ID`) make no request, print nothing, claim nothing —
+unchanged.
+
+## End request (hook → Runtime)
+
+`POST {runtime}/v1/executions/{executionId}/signal` with body `{"signal": <stored signal>}` and headers:
+
+| Header | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `Authorization` | `Bearer <token>` — read from the token file at request time; never printed |
+| `Content-Type` | `application/json` |
+| `X-GraphHelm-Actor` | `<host>-session-<sessionId>` |
+| `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Type` | `agent` |
+| `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session` | `<sessionId>` |
+| `Idempotency-Key` | the stored `delivery.signalId` (`session-end-v2-…`) — identical across retries |
+
+Acceptance requires `ok: true` **and** `data.executionId == execution` **and**
+`data.signalId == signalId`; anything else is a mismatch (unobserved, retryable). Redirects are
+refused; replies are bounded at 256 KiB.
+
+## Timing contract — the changed part of this feature
+
+| Bound | Value | Where | Host that enforces the enclosing process cap |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| SessionStart briefing socket-inactivity limit | 1.0 s (default of `request(...)`) | `session_hook.py` | Claude 5 s / Codex 5 s (SessionStart declarations unchanged) |
+| **SessionEnd acknowledgement socket-inactivity bound** | **`END_ACK_TIMEOUT = 2.0` s** (was an inline 3.0 s literal) | module constant in `session_hook.py` | **Codex 3 s** (declared and enforced); Claude 5 s |
+| Declared Codex SessionEnd timeout | 3 s | both `codex-hooks.json` manifests | Codex host hard cap |
+
+Properties the bound must keep (FR-003/FR-004, SC-002): strictly above the one-second briefing
+limit (a durable acknowledgement slower than one second is still confirmable from the single
+original request — observed by the suite's ~1.25 s-release test), strictly below the three-second
+Codex cap, and sized so interpreter startup + state locking + the durable state write still finish
+inside the cap (sizing evidence: research.md D1; machine-local probe: quickstart.md S4).
+
+Semantics: the bound is **socket inactivity**, not a wall-time deadline; the host's declared
+timeout bounds the whole process. A timeout is an *unobserved* operation — never a success claim
+and never a delivery.
+
+## Delivery, retry, and failure discipline (unchanged by this feature)
+
+Receipt shape and transitions are defined in [../data-model.md](../data-model.md), entity 4. The
+behavioral contract the hook must keep honoring (all covered by the existing suite):
+
+- Acknowledged end → receipt durably marked `delivered`; later end events skip sending (no second POST).
+- Timed-out / refused / mismatched end → state unchanged or undelivered, reported unobserved on
+  stderr without token or path leakage; a later end event retries the **identical signal bytes with
+  the same idempotency key**.
+- Corrupt stored receipt → fails closed: no request until a valid receipt exists again.
+- Overlapping ends → non-blocking lock keeps one signal body; the overlapping caller reports
+  unobserved; the later retry yields exactly one durable delivery.
+- Killed hook → OS releases the lock; retry reuses the stored signal (same bytes, same key).
+
+## Twin-file identity
+
+Both installed copies of `session_hook.py` remain byte-identical (FR-006); the compatibility test
+asserts this on raw bytes, so any drift between copies is a test failure, not a silent split.
