+# Implementation Plan: Fix Codex SessionEnd Hook Budget
+
+**Branch**: `main` (unattended run; `BRANCH=main` from stage setup — no feature branch created) | **Date**: 2026-10-27 | **Spec**: [spec.md](spec.md)
+
+**Input**: Feature specification from `/specs/001-bench/spec.md`
+
+**Governance substitution**: No `/memory/constitution.md` exists in this checkout. Per the run's stage setup, `AGENTS.md` at the repository root is used as the governance reference; the substitution is recorded here, in the spec's Assumptions, and in [research.md](research.md).
+
+## Summary
+
+Codex clamps SessionEnd hook commands to a hard three-second process cap, but both shipped Codex
+hook manifests declare a five-second SessionEnd timeout, and the hook script waits up to
+3.0 seconds of socket inactivity for the Runtime's durable end acknowledgement — so Codex can kill
+the hook mid-wait and lose a slow-but-valid acknowledgement. The fix declares `3` for Codex's
+SessionEnd in both byte-identical manifests, replaces the inline `timeout=3.0` in the end request
+with a named constant `END_ACK_TIMEOUT = 2.0` applied identically to both installed script copies,
+and corrects the budget paragraph in `plugins/graphhelm/README.md`. Claude's manifest keeps its
+five-second budgets, the SessionStart one-second briefing limit is untouched, and the hook contract
+suite (28 tests, baseline green) stays the observer. Technical approach and resolved unknowns are
+in [research.md](research.md).
+
+## Technical Context
+
+**Language/Version**: Python 3.11 (hook script is standard-library-only; `python 3.11.9` verified on this machine). The enclosing repository is a Rust workspace (pinned 1.97.1), but this feature touches no Rust crate.
+
+**Primary Dependencies**: None added. The hook script uses only the Python standard library (`urllib`, `json`, `hashlib`, `tempfile`, `argparse`, `msvcrt`/`fcntl` for the lock). No new dependency may be introduced.
+
+**Storage**: Local-first JSON state files (`version: 2`) plus a sibling `.lock` file under the user's local state directory (`GRAPHHELM_HOOK_STATE_DIR` override supported). Format and migration status are invariant for this feature.
+
+**Testing**: Standard-library `unittest`; single module `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/test_session_hook.py` (28 tests) driving the hook via subprocesses against an in-process `ThreadingHTTPServer` on loopback. No network, Docker, credentials, or browser sessions needed. Baseline measured on this machine: 28/28 pass, exit 0.
+
+**Target Platform**: Windows (this checkout; PowerShell 5.1-compatible commands) and POSIX. Hosts: Codex CLI (three-second SessionEnd cap, owner-provided fact) and Claude Code (five-second bound).
+
+**Project Type**: Agent-host command-hook plugin shipped inside a Rust-workspace repository; this feature modifies only plugin artifacts (two JSON manifests, two script copies, one README paragraph).
+
+**Performance Goals**: Worst in-window end run — acknowledgement arriving arbitrarily close to the 2.0 s inactivity bound — completes in under three seconds of wall time, with interpreter startup, state locking, and the durable state write included (headroom analysis in research.md, D1).
+
+**Constraints**: Declared Codex SessionEnd timeout must equal the host cap (3); the end-acknowledgement socket-inactivity bound must be strictly inside (1.0, 3.0) seconds (SC-002) and sized with overhead headroom (FR-003); the two script copies and the two Codex manifests must remain byte-identical pairs (FR-006, enforced by the compatibility test); the token never appears in stdout/stderr/signals; English-only documentation; no ceremonial stubs or TODOs.
+
