+# Research: Fix Codex SessionEnd Hook Budget
+
+**Feature**: [spec.md](spec.md) | **Date**: 2026-10-27 | **Governance reference**: `AGENTS.md` (constitution substitute — no `/memory/constitution.md` in this checkout)
+
+Phase 0 resolves every open question the Technical Context left to planning. There are no
+`NEEDS CLARIFICATION` markers in the spec (unattended run; open decisions were carried into the
+spec's Assumptions) and this file records how each was resolved here, with alternatives and the
+measured grounding for each choice. All facts below were verified in this checkout on 2026-10-27.
+
+## Grounding evidence (measured before deciding)
+
+| Fact | Value | How measured |
+|------|-------|--------------|
+| Python available for the observer | 3.11.9 | `python --version` |
+| Baseline hook suite | 28 tests, OK, exit 0, ~8.2 s | `python -m unittest plugins.graphhelm.hooks.test_session_hook -v` |
+| `session_hook.py` twin pair byte-identical | SHA-256 `67A7B1CE932E9EAF58FBACF0442D32C0F13ACA3C213D402B3D60C57DB9D9E895` (both copies) | `Get-FileHash` on both copies |
+| Codex manifest twin pair byte-identical | SHA-256 `B671BBE63B326D7D07E898A1850B1F8DF5480D83608FE31A3A37F0E4CB1BC596` (both copies) | `Get-FileHash` on both copies |
+| Claude manifest baseline (must stay unchanged) | SHA-256 `5FA2B622D3B35AE4BD40E07B49F0C319BA630699A6C2813CE5E1A684E758FB4D` | `Get-FileHash` |
+| Declared Codex SessionEnd timeout | `5` at line 23 of both `codex-hooks.json` | file read |
+| End-request wait | `timeout=3.0` inline at `session_hook.py:351` (`_end_impl`) | file read |
+| SessionStart briefing wait | `request(...)` default `timeout: float = 1.0`, used unmodified by the briefing GET | file read |
+| Slow-ack test behavior | `test_end_accepts_slow_matching_ack_without_extra_posts` releases a real HTTP response ~1.25 s after the POST begins and asserts delivery from one request | test source, lines 245–274 |
+| Budget figures elsewhere in the repo | Only `plugins/graphhelm/README.md` lines 57–61 carry them in shipped artifacts (other "seconds"/"timeout" hits are unrelated) | repository-wide case-insensitive grep over `plugins/` |
+| Compatibility pins already enforced by tests | `test_codex_compatibility_package_keeps_one_hook_source`: raw-byte identity of both script copies and both Codex manifests, equal plugin versions across all four `plugin.json` files, hooks pointer `./hooks/codex-hooks.json`, no `extensions` key, no root `plugin.json` in the companion | test source, lines 103–124 |
+
+## D1 — Exact end-acknowledgement socket-inactivity bound
+
+**Decision**: `END_ACK_TIMEOUT = 2.0` seconds.
+
+**Rationale**: The spec's bounds (FR-003, FR-004, SC-002) fix an open interval plus a sizing duty:
+strictly above the one-second briefing limit, strictly below the three-second Codex cap, with
+headroom so interpreter startup, state locking, and the durable state write still fit inside the
+cap. The tightest *observed* demand on the bound is the contract test that releases a real
+acknowledgement ~1.25 s after the POST begins — the bound must absorb that plus timer/thread
+jitter. Wall-time arithmetic for the worst in-window case (ack arrives arbitrarily close to the
+bound): CPython startup for this stdlib-only 453-line script (~30–150 ms) + loopback connect +
+up to 2.0 s inactivity + post-ack parse/validate + one `fsync`'d state write (typically tens of ms
+on this machine) ≈ 2.2–2.5 s, leaving ≥ 0.5 s under the hard cap. 2.0 s also leaves 0.75 s of
+margin above the test's 1.25 s release point, keeping the suite green without making it more
+timing-sensitive than it already is. The value is the spec's own stated reasonable default, which
+keeps plan and assumption consistent.
+
+**Alternatives considered**:
+
+- `1.5 s` — only ~0.25 s above the test's 1.25 s release; a loaded machine (timer jitter, slow
+  thread wakeup in the in-process server) could push the observed response past the bound and turn
+  a contract test flaky. Rejected: buys little headroom under the cap, spends most of the margin
+  the suite needs.
+- `1.75 s` — same class of risk, slightly larger. Rejected.
+- `2.25 s` / `2.5 s` — grows the wall-time worst case toward 2.4–2.9 s against a hard 3.0 s kill;
+  a single slow `fsync` or cold-interpreter startup on a busy host would consume the remaining
+  headroom. Rejected: the defect being fixed is precisely a host killing a slow hook.
+- Per-host bound (e.g., 4 s on Claude, 2 s on Codex) — Claude's five-second cap would allow more,
+  but would require host branching inside `_end_impl`, a larger diff surface, and an unmeasured
+  benefit: acknowledgement latency is a Runtime property, not a host property, and the compatibility
+  contract wants one identical script body. Rejected.
+
+## D2 — Where the bound lives in code
+
+**Decision**: A module-level constant `END_ACK_TIMEOUT = 2.0` added to the existing bounds block
+near the other module constants (`MAX_INPUT`, …, `MAX_CONTEXT`) with a short comment stating the
+budget relationship (above the 1.0 s briefing limit, strictly below the 3 s Codex cap, sized for
+overhead headroom), and `_end_impl`'s call site changed from the inline literal `timeout=3.0` to
+`timeout=END_ACK_TIMEOUT`. Both installed copies receive the identical edit.
+
+**Rationale**: The spec requires the chosen value to be "recorded where implemented"; a named
+constant next to the other bounds makes the budget relationship readable at the definition site,
+greppable, and byte-stable across the twin copies. It is the only new public surface the change
+introduces (named in the Keel card).
+
+**Alternatives considered**:
+
+- Keep an inline literal `2.0` at the call site — smaller diff, but the budget rationale would live
+  only in a comment beside a magic number and SC-002 ("maximum end-acknowledgement wait is …") has
+  no single addressable location. Rejected.
+- Add a host parameter or argument (`--end-timeout`) — new interface surface with no consumer; the
+  host does not receive this value from us (the host enforces its own cap). Rejected as scaffold.
+
+The comment block inside `request()` ("This is socket inactivity, not a wall-time guarantee. The
+host hook timeout bounds the whole process; …") remains accurate after the change and is not
+edited, minimizing churn in a byte-identity-critical file.
+
+## D3 — Manifest edit shape
+
+**Decision**: In each `codex-hooks.json`, change exactly the SessionEnd entry's `"timeout": 5` to
+`"timeout": 3`. No reformatting; every other byte (including trailing newline state and key order)
+is preserved, and both copies receive the same edit so the raw-byte equality test keeps passing.
+
+**Rationale**: FR-001 demands the declared value; US2-AS1 demands every other entry untouched;
+FR-006 demands byte-identical twins. A single-token edit is the minimal diff satisfying all three,
+and hash comparison after the edit proves twin identity independent of the test.
+
+**Alternatives considered**: Parse-and-reserialize JSON (normalizes formatting — risks a diff
+churn larger than the requirement and a non-minimal change); editing only one copy and letting the
+installer sync (there is no installer in this repository — the copies are the source). Rejected.
+
+## D4 — README correction scope
+
+**Decision**: Correct exactly the two stale statements in the Session-hooks budget paragraph
+(`plugins/graphhelm/README.md`, lines 57–61):
+
+1. "SessionEnd allows one request up to **three seconds** of socket inactivity" → the corrected
+   two-second bound, preserving the sentence's point (a durable acknowledgement slower than one
+   second is still confirmed without resending).
+2. "**Both hosts bound the SessionEnd command process to five seconds.**" → replaced by the true
+   split: Codex caps the SessionEnd command process at three seconds (which the Codex manifests now
+   declare), while Claude keeps its five-second bound, and the end-acknowledgement wait is sized to
+   complete inside the Codex cap.
+
+The surrounding sentences ("SessionStart keeps its one-second socket inactivity limit.", "The
+socket limit alone is not a wall-time deadline. A timeout remains unobserved; it never becomes task
+success.") remain verbatim.
+
+**Rationale**: FR-007 names these two defects and the repository-wide sweep (grounding table above)
+confirms no other shipped document carries these figures, so the paragraph is the entire prose
+surface. Smallest edit that makes every number in the paragraph equal the shipped manifests
+(SC-005).
+
+**Alternatives considered**: Rewrite the whole paragraph (unnecessary churn in a reviewed guide);
+leave "three seconds" and note the discrepancy elsewhere (defeats the purpose — the reader must
+find figures matching the manifests). Rejected.
+
+## D5 — Compatibility-contract invariants the change must respect
+
+**Decision**: Treat as frozen until proven otherwise: all four `plugin.json` files (versions must
+stay equal; companion hooks pointer stays `./hooks/codex-hooks.json`; no `extensions` key; no root
+`plugin.json` in the companion), the full Claude manifest (baseline SHA recorded above), the
+matcher/statusMessage/context-limit/command strings in both Codex manifests, and the test module
+itself.
+
+**Rationale**: The compatibility test pins all of these on raw bytes and values; they are the
+shipped contract between the main plugin and the companion package (US2). Nothing in D1–D4 needs
+to touch them, so they stay out of scope and the unchanged Claude-manifest SHA doubles as a cheap
+post-change check.
+
+**Alternatives considered**: Bumping plugin versions for the manifest change — the version pin is a
+compatibility invariant, not a content hash; the spec nowhere requires a version move and moving
+it would widen the diff beyond the promise. Rejected.
+
