+# Feature Specification: Fix Codex SessionEnd Hook Budget
+
+**Feature Branch**: n/a (unattended run; no branch created by this command)
+
+**Created**: 2026-10-27
+
+**Status**: Draft
+
+**Input**: User description: "Installed Codex clamps a `SessionEnd` hook command to three seconds, but both Codex hook files (`plugins/graphhelm/hooks/codex-hooks.json` and `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/codex-hooks.json`) declare 5, and the end request in `session_hook.py` waits up to 3.0 seconds of socket inactivity, so Codex can kill the hook while it is still waiting. Declare 3 for Codex's SessionEnd and make the end acknowledgement wait fit inside it (both copies of `session_hook.py`). Claude's `hooks.json` keeps its 5-second budget. Correct the budget paragraph in `plugins/graphhelm/README.md`. The hook tests must stay green."
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*
+
+### User Story 1 - The end acknowledgement survives the host kill (Priority: P1)
+
+A user who has bound a Codex session to a GraphHelm execution ends the session. Codex kills any
+SessionEnd hook process that reaches its three-second cap. Today the end hook declares a five-second
+budget that Codex does not honor, and it waits up to three seconds of socket inactivity for the
+Runtime's durable end acknowledgement — so the host can terminate the hook in the middle of that wait.
+A slow-but-valid acknowledgement is then lost from that run, even though the retry machinery exists.
+After this feature, the Codex hook declares the budget Codex actually enforces (three seconds), and
+the end acknowledgement wait is short enough that the whole hook finishes inside that cap. An
+acknowledgement slower than the one-second briefing limit is still confirmed without resending the
+signal; if the acknowledgement does not arrive in time, nothing is claimed delivered and the next end
+event retries the same signal with the same idempotency key.
+
+**Why this priority**: This is the defect itself. Every other statement in this spec exists so Codex
+users get the acknowledgement behavior the plugin already promises them — but only within a budget the
+host actually grants.
+
+**Independent Test**: Run the plugin's hook contract suite (the single Python test module that drives
+the hook script against an in-process Runtime); it verifies acknowledged, slow, refused, mismatched,
+overlapping, and killed-hook end flows. Additionally, read the shipped manifests and the hook script to
+confirm the declared Codex SessionEnd budget is three seconds and the end-acknowledgement wait is
+strictly inside it.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a Codex host that caps SessionEnd hooks at three seconds, and the installed Codex hook
+   manifest declares a three-second SessionEnd timeout, **When** a bound session ends and the Runtime
+   acknowledges the end signal while the hook waits, **Then** the hook finishes inside the three-second
+   budget and the local delivery state records the confirmation.
+2. **Given** the Runtime needs more than one second but less than the end-acknowledgement wait bound to
+   produce a matching acknowledgement, **When** the end hook runs, **Then** the acknowledgement is
+   accepted from the single original request — no duplicate signal is sent.
+3. **Given** the Runtime does not acknowledge before the wait bound elapses, **When** the end hook
+   runs, **Then** the hook exits cleanly with the operation reported unobserved (no success claim),
+   local state is not marked delivered, and a later end event retries the identical signal body with
+   the same idempotency key.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 2 - Declared budgets match the effective host budgets (Priority: P2)
+
+A maintainer (or anyone auditing the plugin) reads the hook registration files and sees one
+consistent story instead of a budget the host quietly strips away: both Codex hook manifests declare a
+three-second SessionEnd budget, equal to the Codex cap, and the Claude host manifest keeps its existing
+five-second budget. The two Codex manifests and the two installed copies of the hook script — which the
+compatibility contract requires to be byte-identical to each other — stay identical through the change.
+
+**Why this priority**: Without it the fix is untruthful: a declared-five budget on Codex advertises a
+window the host never grants, and drift between the twin files breaks the shipped compatibility
+contract.
+
+**Independent Test**: Read both Codex hook manifests and the Claude hook manifest from the repository;
+verify Codex SessionEnd declares three seconds with every other entry untouched, Claude still declares
+five seconds, and the paired Codex manifests compare byte-for-byte identical.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the two shipped Codex hook manifests, **When** they are read, **Then** each declares a
+   three-second SessionEnd timeout and every other entry (SessionStart timeout, matcher, status
+   message, context limit, commands) is unchanged.
+2. **Given** the Claude hook manifest, **When** it is read, **Then** its SessionEnd timeout remains
+   five seconds and no other value changed.
+3. **Given** the compatibility contract between the main plugin and the Codex companion package,
+   **When** the change is applied, **Then** the two Codex manifests remain byte-identical to each other
+   and the two hook-script copies remain byte-identical to each other.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 3 - The documented budget tells the true budget (Priority: P3)
+
+A reader of the plugin guide relies on it to learn how long session-end processing may take. After this
+feature, the guide's budget paragraph describes the corrected reality: Codex caps the SessionEnd command
+process at three seconds (and the Codex manifests now declare exactly that), Claude keeps its
+five-second bound, and the end-acknowledgement wait is sized to complete inside the Codex cap. The old
+sentence claiming both hosts bound the SessionEnd command process to five seconds is gone.
+
+**Why this priority**: Documentation accuracy prevents the next reader from re-deriving the old,
+incorrect budget from stale prose; it carries no runtime risk of its own.
+
+**Independent Test**: Read the Session-hooks budget paragraph in the plugin README and compare each
+number against the shipped manifests; the two must agree.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the corrected manifests, **When** the plugin README's Session-hooks budget paragraph is
+   read, **Then** it states the three-second Codex SessionEnd cap, the unchanged five-second Claude
+   bound, and that the end-acknowledgement wait fits inside the Codex cap, and it no longer states that
+   both hosts bound the SessionEnd command process to five seconds.
+
+### Edge Cases
+
+- **Acknowledgement arrives just after the new (shorter) wait but before the host kill**: the request
+  times out and the operation is unobserved; state is not marked delivered; the next end event retries
+  with identical signal bytes. A partially-delivered state must never be observable.
+- **Two end events overlap while the first is still inside its request**: the existing lock keeps one
+  signal body; the overlapping caller reports unobserved and the later retry yields exactly one durable
+  delivery (already covered by the hook contract tests).
+- **Unbound session**: no execution binding configured — the end hook makes no request, prints nothing,
+  and claims nothing, exactly as today.
+- **Refused or mismatched acknowledgement**: remains retryable and unobserved; the token value never
+  appears in the signal, on stdout, or on stderr.
+- **Corrupt stored delivery receipt**: still fails closed — no request is sent until a valid receipt
+  exists again.
+- **Twin-file integrity**: the hook script has two installed copies and the Codex manifest has two
+  copies; a change that lands in only one copy violates the compatibility contract and must be caught
+  by the hook tests.
+
+## Requirements *(mandatory)*
+
+### Functional Requirements
+
+- **FR-001**: The system MUST declare a three-second SessionEnd hook timeout in both Codex hook
+  manifests (`plugins/graphhelm/hooks/codex-hooks.json` and
+  `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/codex-hooks.json`), matching the Codex host cap.
+- **FR-002**: The system MUST leave the Claude host manifest (`plugins/graphhelm/hooks/hooks.json`)
+  unchanged: its SessionEnd timeout remains five seconds, as does its SessionStart timeout.
+- **FR-003**: The system MUST reduce the SessionEnd acknowledgement wait in the hook script to a
+  bounded socket-inactivity limit strictly below three seconds, sized so that interpreter startup,
+  state locking, and the durable state write still complete inside the three-second Codex process cap.
+- **FR-004**: The system MUST keep the SessionEnd acknowledgement wait long enough that a durable
+  acknowledgement slower than the one-second briefing limit is still confirmed without resending the
+  signal (the existing contract behavior releases a real acknowledgement past the one-second boundary
+  and requires it to be accepted).
+- **FR-005**: The system MUST preserve unobserved-and-retryable semantics: a timed-out, refused, or
+  mismatched end request leaves the signal undelivered, reports the operation unobserved without a
+  success claim, and a later end event retries the identical signal body with the same idempotency key.
+- **FR-006**: The system MUST apply the hook-script change identically to both installed copies
+  (`plugins/graphhelm/hooks/session_hook.py` and
+  `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/session_hook.py`) so the pair remains byte-identical, and keep
+  the two Codex manifests byte-identical to each other.
+- **FR-007**: The system MUST correct the Session-hooks budget paragraph in
+  `plugins/graphhelm/README.md` so that the documented budgets equal the effective ones: the three
+  second Codex SessionEnd cap (now declared in the Codex manifests), the unchanged five-second Claude
+  bound, and the end-acknowledgement wait sized to fit inside the Codex cap; the sentence claiming both
+  hosts bound the SessionEnd command process to five seconds MUST be removed or corrected.
+- **FR-008**: The existing hook contract test suite MUST remain fully green after the change, with no
+  skipped or failing tests.
+
+### Key Entities
+
+- **Codex hook manifest**: registers the SessionStart/SessionEnd hook commands for the Codex host,
+  including the declared per-event timeout. Carried in two byte-identical copies (main plugin and Codex
+  companion package). Its SessionEnd timeout changes from five to three seconds; all other attributes
+  are invariant.
+- **Claude hook manifest**: the corresponding registration for the Claude host. Invariant: five-second
+  timeouts on both events.
+- **Session hook script (two installed copies)**: the local-first script that reads the start briefing
+  and posts the attributed end signal. Holds the socket-inactivity bound used by the end
+  acknowledgement request; the copies must remain byte-identical.
+- **Budget paragraph (plugin README)**: the prose describing the SessionStart/SessionEnd timing
+  budgets. Updated to match the shipped manifests.
+
+## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*
+
+### Measurable Outcomes
+
+- **SC-001**: Zero declared-versus-effective budget mismatch for Codex: both Codex hook manifests
+  declare a three-second SessionEnd budget (equal to the Codex cap), remain byte-identical to each
+  other, and the Claude manifest still declares five seconds.
+- **SC-002**: In both installed hook scripts, the maximum end-acknowledgement wait is strictly below
+  three seconds and strictly above the one-second briefing limit.
+- **SC-003**: The complete hook contract suite passes with zero failures and zero skips (baseline on
+  this machine: 28 tests, all passing, measured 2026-10-27).
+- **SC-004**: A durable acknowledgement taking longer than one second is still confirmed from a single
+  end request within the Codex three-second budget; when the acknowledgement misses the window, the
+  signal remains retryable with identical bytes and no success is claimed.
+- **SC-005**: A reader of the plugin README finds budget figures matching the shipped manifests
+  (Codex three seconds, Claude five seconds) and no remaining statement that both hosts bound the
+  SessionEnd command process to five seconds.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- Codex enforces a hard three-second process cap on SessionEnd hook commands regardless of the declared
+  value (owner-provided fact; the observed failure mode is the host killing the hook mid-wait). The
+  five-second declaration is therefore not honored by Codex, while Claude's five-second declaration
+  stands.
+- "Fits inside" the three-second cap is interpreted as: the end-acknowledgement socket-inactivity bound
+  plus worst-case process overhead (interpreter startup, state locking, durable state write) completes
+  within three seconds of wall time. The exact constant is an implementation choice constrained by
+  FR-003 and FR-004 (strictly above the one-second briefing limit, strictly below three seconds, with
+  headroom for overhead); the chosen value is recorded where implemented and applied identically to
+  both script copies. Reasonable default: 2.0 seconds.
+- The SessionStart budgets are out of scope: the one-second briefing socket limit and the declared
+  five-second SessionStart timeouts in all three manifests remain unchanged.
+- No change to the signal schema, local state format, or idempotency key is introduced; existing local
+  state needs no migration, and delivery deduplication semantics are unchanged.
+- Observability of this feature is local to the plugin: the hook contract suite (one Python test
+  module, in-process Runtime, no network or credentials needed) plus direct inspection of the shipped
+  JSON manifests and README. Workspace-wide builds and the full Rust test matrix are not required to
+  observe this change.
+- This run is unattended: no clarifying questions were posed; open decisions were resolved with the
+  assumptions above and are recorded here.
+- Where the workflow references the project constitution (`/memory/constitution.md`), no constitution
+  file exists in this checkout; the `AGENTS.md` file at the repository root was used as the governance
+  reference instead, and this substitution is recorded here. Its constraints most relevant to this
+  feature: English-only documentation, every public method must have working behavior in the same
+  commit (no ceremonial stubs), and Keel proportionality — this change is a bounded config-plus-code
+  edit on named paths with a known observer, so it follows the direct route with a three-line card
+  (paths, promise, observing command) at planning time.
+- The feature directory name `specs/001-bench` is fixed by the run harness and is unrelated to the
+  feature name.
